\section{From the Pilot to the Deployment}

% --- M0.1 The access problem and the pilot's restraint (batch 1, 2026-07-19)

Clinical immersion is scarce by structure. The programs that admit undergraduates
to operating rooms and clinical settings draw substantially more applicants than
they can serve, so that a majority of the students who seek a clinical-immersion
experience will not have one---an inequity the biomedical-engineering literature
documents against a clear demand.\footnote{Christine E. King and Dalton Salvo,
``Phenomenological Evaluation of an Undergraduate Clinical Needs Finding Skills
Through a Virtual Reality Clinical Immersion Platform,'' \textit{Biomedical
Engineering Education} 4 (2024): 381--97, at 381; reproduced as
Appendix~\ref{app:vr-pilot}.} The virtual clinical-immersion platform whose
deployment this section reads was built against that scarcity: filmed operations,
physician and engineer interviews, and explorable procedure rooms, delivered so
that any enrolled student could stand where few can be brought to stand. Its
first published test came in the spring of 2022, when a pilot cohort of
twenty-two met the same operating-room footage in four competing formats---plain
video on YouTube, an interactive computer build, a phone-in-cardboard viewer, and
a full headset---and, across twenty post-course responses, returned a two-part
verdict that would not line up. Presence went to the headset: seventy percent
reported that the virtual-reality version elicited a greater felt sense of
presence. Preference went the other way: forty percent judged the plain videos
most educationally beneficial---as large a share as both headset routes
combined---on the stated grounds of convenience and physical discomfort; and of
the loanable headsets housed in the library, ``not a single student went there to
check one out,'' because, as one student put it, ``it was a
hassle.''\footnote{King and Salvo, ``Phenomenological Evaluation,'' 388--89.}

What the article did with that record matters as much as the record itself. It
declined the verdict its own design seemed to promise: because the course's
commitment to open access let every student default to the most convenient
format, the four versions never competed on equal grounds, and the authors drew
the boundary plainly---while a majority of students undeniably felt more present
in virtual reality, they ``cannot conclusively claim that interactive VR learning
environments are more or less pedagogically efficacious compared to 2D videos at
this point in time.''\footnote{King and Salvo, ``Phenomenological Evaluation,''
390.} The pilot thus established two things and guarded the line between them:
presence was achieved, and preference went elsewhere. Whether the world
teaches---whether felt presence converts into pedagogical worth---was left
standing, deliberately, as a question the pilot's own conditions could not
answer. The study its restraint called for is a study of the world at scale, in
the life of the course itself; three terms of deployment have since supplied that
record, and reading it is this section's work.

